        "In 1977, astrophysicists Lucy and Monaghan invented Smoothed Particle Hydrodynamics (SPH) to simulate colliding stars and interstellar gas clouds. In 2003, Matthias M\u00fcller and colleagues adapted SPH for real-time computer graphics. In SPH, a continuous fluid volume is discretized into tens of thousands of moving particles. Each particle i carries a fixed mass m, a position x_i, a velocity v_i, and a finite radius of influence called the Smoothing Kernel Radius h.",
        "To compute continuous fluid properties at any spatial point without a fixed computational grid, SPH evaluates a radial smoothing kernel W(r, h). To compute the density rho_i at particle i, the simulation sums the smoothed mass contributions of all neighboring particles within radius h: rho_i = sum_j m_j * W(||x_i - x_j||, h). For density interpolation, graphics engines deploy the famous M\u00fcller Poly6 Smoothing Kernel: W_poly6(r, h) = 315 / (64 * pi * h^9) * (h^2 - r^2)^3 for r <= h, and 0 for r > h.",
